@@ -0,0 +1,217 @@
+# json-patch Specification
+
+## Purpose
+TBD - created by archiving change add-json-patch. Update Purpose after archive.
+
+## Requirements
+
+### Requirement: Patch structure
+Per RFC 6902 §3, a patch SHALL be an array of operation objects, each with an `op` member naming one of `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test` and a string `path` member holding a JSON Pointer. Members not defined for the operation MUST be ignored (§4). Operations SHALL be applied sequentially, each to the result of the previous one (§5). An invalid patch MUST cause an error to be thrown (§4, §5), before any result is returned.
+
+#### Scenario: Empty patch
+- **WHEN** the patch is `[]`
+- **THEN** the result equals the document
+
+#### Scenario: Patch is not an array
+- **WHEN** the patch is an object, `null`, a string or a number
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Operation is malformed
+- **WHEN** an operation is not an object (e.g. `null`, an array, a string)
+- **OR** `op` is missing, not a string, or not one of the six names (including different case, e.g. `"Add"`)
+- **OR** `path` is missing or not a string
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Required members missing
+- **WHEN** `add`, `replace` or `test` has no `value` member, or `move` or `copy` has no string `from` member
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+- **AND** `"value": null` is a present value and is accepted
+
+#### Scenario: Unknown extra members
+- **WHEN** an operation has extra members, e.g. `{"op":"remove","path":"/a","comment":"x"}`
+- **THEN** they are ignored
+
+#### Scenario: Invalid pointer
+- **WHEN** a `path` or `from` is not a valid JSON Pointer (e.g. `"a"`, or a bad escape such as `"/a~2"`)
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Later operation fails
+- **WHEN** the first operation succeeds and the second fails
+- **THEN** an error is thrown and no result is returned
+- **AND** the original document is unchanged
+
+### Requirement: Inputs are not mutated and results are not aliased
+`applyPatch` SHALL NOT mutate `document` or `patch`. The result SHALL NOT share any object or array with `document` or `patch`, so later mutation of the result cannot affect either input.
+
+#### Scenario: Document not mutated
+- **WHEN** a patch with `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move` and `copy` operations is applied to a nested document
+- **THEN** a deep copy of the document taken beforehand is still deeply equal to it
+
+#### Scenario: Patch values not aliased
+- **WHEN** an `add` value is an object and the result is later mutated
+- **THEN** the object in the patch is unchanged
+- **AND** a `copy` of that value yields an independent object, not a shared reference
+
+### Requirement: add
+Per RFC 6902 §4.1, `add` SHALL work by the type of the parent of the target location:
+- the whole document, if `path` is `""` (the result is `value`);
+- an array: `value` is inserted before the given index, shifting later elements; index equal to the array length, or `-`, appends; an index greater than the length MUST be an error; the index must follow RFC 6901 §4 (no leading zeros, no sign);
+- an object: a new member is added; an existing member is replaced.
+The parent of the target MUST exist; otherwise an error SHALL be thrown.
+
+#### Scenario: Add object member
+- **WHEN** `{"op":"add","path":"/b","value":2}` is applied to `{"a":1}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"a":1,"b":2}`
+
+#### Scenario: Add replaces existing object member
+- **WHEN** `{"op":"add","path":"/a","value":9}` is applied to `{"a":1}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"a":9}`
+
+#### Scenario: Add into array
+- **WHEN** `add` at `/1` of value `"x"` is applied to `["a","b"]`
+- **THEN** the result is `["a","x","b"]`
+- **AND** at `/0` it is prepended, at `/2` (the length) and at `/-` it is appended
+
+#### Scenario: Add array index errors
+- **WHEN** the index is greater than the array length (e.g. `/3` on a 2-element array), is `01`, `-1`, `+1`, `1.0`, or is not a number (e.g. `/a`)
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Add whole document
+- **WHEN** `{"op":"add","path":"","value":[1]}` is applied to any document
+- **THEN** the result is `[1]`
+
+#### Scenario: Missing parent
+- **WHEN** `add` at `/a/b` is applied to `{}`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Parent is a scalar
+- **WHEN** `add` at `/a/b` is applied to `{"a":1}` or `{"a":null}`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Empty-string and escaped keys
+- **WHEN** `add` at `/` (key `""`), at `/a~1b` (key `a/b`) and at `/m~0n` (key `m~n`) is applied to `{}`
+- **THEN** the result has exactly those keys `""`, `"a/b"`, `"m~n"`
+
+#### Scenario: Hostile key
+- **WHEN** `add` at `/__proto__` with value `{"polluted":true}` is applied to `{}`
+- **THEN** the result has an own member named `__proto__` with that value
+- **AND** `Object.prototype` is not modified and `({}).polluted` is `undefined`
+
+### Requirement: remove
+Per RFC 6902 §4.2, `remove` SHALL remove the value at the target location, which MUST exist. Removing from an array shifts later elements down. Removing the whole document (`path` `""`) is not defined by the RFC and SHALL be an error.
+
+#### Scenario: Remove object member
+- **WHEN** `remove` at `/a` is applied to `{"a":1,"b":2}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"b":2}`
+
+#### Scenario: Remove array element
+- **WHEN** `remove` at `/1` is applied to `["a","b","c"]`
+- **THEN** the result is `["a","c"]`
+
+#### Scenario: Remove nonexistent target
+- **WHEN** `remove` targets a missing member, an index equal to or beyond the array length, `-`, or an inherited name such as `/toString`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Remove whole document
+- **WHEN** `remove` at `""` is applied
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+### Requirement: replace
+Per RFC 6902 §4.3, `replace` SHALL replace the value at the target location, which MUST exist (semantically `remove` then `add`, but a replaced array element keeps its position). `path` `""` replaces the whole document.
+
+#### Scenario: Replace object member and array element
+- **WHEN** `replace` at `/a` with `2` is applied to `{"a":1}`, and at `/1` with `"x"` to `["a","b","c"]`
+- **THEN** the results are `{"a":2}` and `["a","x","c"]`
+
+#### Scenario: Replace nonexistent target
+- **WHEN** `replace` targets a missing member, an array index beyond the last element, or `-`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Replace whole document
+- **WHEN** `replace` at `""` with `null` is applied
+- **THEN** the result is `null`
+
+### Requirement: move
+Per RFC 6902 §4.4, `move` SHALL remove the value at `from` (which MUST exist) and add it at `path`, as `remove` followed by `add` of that value. `from` MUST NOT be a proper prefix of `path` (a location cannot be moved into one of its own children); this SHALL be an error. A prefix is compared by whole reference tokens, not by characters.
+
+#### Scenario: Move object member
+- **WHEN** `{"op":"move","from":"/a","path":"/b"}` is applied to `{"a":1}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"b":1}`
+
+#### Scenario: Move within an array
+- **WHEN** `move` from `/0` to `/2` is applied to `["a","b","c"]`
+- **THEN** the result is `["b","c","a"]`, because the index of `path` is evaluated after the removal
+- **AND** from `/2` to `/0` yields `["c","a","b"]`
+
+#### Scenario: Move to the same location
+- **WHEN** `from` equals `path` and the location exists
+- **THEN** the document is unchanged
+
+#### Scenario: Move into own child
+- **WHEN** `move` from `/a` to `/a/b` is applied to `{"a":{}}`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Similar names are not prefixes
+- **WHEN** `move` from `/a` to `/ab` is applied to `{"a":1}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"ab":1}`
+
+#### Scenario: Move from nonexistent location
+- **WHEN** `from` does not exist
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Move of the whole document
+- **WHEN** `from` is `""` and `path` is non-empty
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+### Requirement: copy
+Per RFC 6902 §4.5, `copy` SHALL add a copy of the value at `from` (which MUST exist) at `path`, with `add` semantics. The copy SHALL be independent of the source.
+
+#### Scenario: Copy value
+- **WHEN** `{"op":"copy","from":"/a","path":"/b"}` is applied to `{"a":{"x":[1]}}`
+- **THEN** the result is `{"a":{"x":[1]},"b":{"x":[1]}}`
+- **AND** the two values are distinct objects
+
+#### Scenario: Copy into array
+- **WHEN** `copy` from `/0` to `/-` is applied to `["a","b"]`
+- **THEN** the result is `["a","b","a"]`
+
+#### Scenario: Copy from nonexistent location
+- **WHEN** `from` does not exist
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+### Requirement: test
+Per RFC 6902 §4.6, `test` SHALL succeed only if the value at `path` (which MUST exist) is equal to `value`, and otherwise an error SHALL be thrown. Equality: same type; strings equal by code points; numbers equal by numeric value (`1` and `1.0` are equal); `null`, `true`, `false` equal to themselves; arrays equal if same length and elements equal pairwise in order; objects equal if they have the same set of member names and equal values, regardless of order. `test` does not change the document.
+
+#### Scenario: Passing tests
+- **WHEN** `test` compares equal scalars, `null`, arrays, nested objects, or objects with members in a different order
+- **THEN** it succeeds and the result equals the document
+
+#### Scenario: Failing tests
+- **WHEN** values differ in type (`1` vs `"1"`, `null` vs `0`, `0` vs `false`, `[]` vs `{}`), in array order or length, or in object member names or values
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Test of nonexistent location
+- **WHEN** `path` does not exist, even if `value` is `null`
+- **THEN** a `JsonPatchError` is thrown
+
+#### Scenario: Test whole document
+- **WHEN** `test` at `""` compares to an equal value
+- **THEN** it succeeds
+
+#### Scenario: Test guards later operations
+- **WHEN** a failing `test` is followed by an `add`
+- **THEN** an error is thrown and no result is returned
+
+### Requirement: Errors
+All failures (invalid patch, invalid pointer, failed operation) SHALL be thrown as `JsonPatchError`, an `Error` subclass with `name` `"JsonPatchError"`.
+
+#### Scenario: Error type
+- **WHEN** any invalid patch or failed operation occurs
+- **THEN** the thrown value is an instance of `JsonPatchError` and of `Error`
+
+### Requirement: RFC 6902 Appendix A examples
+The implementation SHALL produce the documented results for the RFC 6902 Appendix A examples A.1–A.16.
+
+#### Scenario: Appendix A
+- **WHEN** each example's document and patch are applied
+- **THEN** the result equals the documented result, or an error is thrown where the RFC documents an error (A.12, A.13, A.15, A.16)
